@namespace Burbuja.Portal.Components.Layout
@inject PortalData Data
@implements IDisposable

@* M12 R1 — the rail. Grouped the way an operator looks for things — what is happening, what needs a
   decision, what this host serves — rather than alphabetically, because the question somebody arrives
   with is one of those three and never "what starts with M".
 *
 * Every row carries an inline-SVG icon and a label. The icon is not decoration: it is what survives the
 * collapse to a 56px strip, and it is what makes a row findable at a glance once a reader has used the
 * console twice. The label stays in the DOM when collapsed (hidden by CSS) so the link keeps its
 * accessible name — a collapsed rail of unlabelled links is a rail a screen reader cannot navigate.
 *
 * M14 R5p — THE GROUPS ARE FILES. Each one is a `Rail*.razor` component, so four lanes never edit this
 * file: a lane with a new row edits `RailAkos.razor` (or appends its own group component here, one line).
 * The `@if (_pomnibot)` guard below stays here because it is about the CALLER's roles, not about a group.
 *@
<nav class="nav rail-nav" aria-label="Sections">
    <a class="kernel-lockup" href="/portal" aria-label="Burbuja, home">
        <span class="kernel-lockup-mark"><Icon Name="logo" Size="40" /></span>
        <span class="kernel-lockup-text">
            <span class="kernel-lockup-name">Burbuja</span>
            <span class="kernel-lockup-sub">Kernel operations</span>
        </span>
    </a>
    @* M16 — the AKOS group is GONE from this rail. M15 M-UI (item 2) had put it first, because the question
       this console existed to answer was "what is AKOS doing"; M16 answered that by giving AKOS its own
       portal at `/akos`, with its own shell, its own door and its own rail (`AkosNavMenu`, which renders
       `<RailAkos />`). This is the operator's console: Home / Monitor / Operate / Catalogue are its
       sections, and its rows are English. The component itself is unchanged and shared — a lane's new AKOS
       row still lands in both rails or in neither. *@

    @* lanes: append below — a lane's own group component goes on this line, before RailCatalogue. It sat
       immediately after the AKOS group before M15 item 2 lifted AKOS to the top, and it stays there: a lane's
       group is a workload group, so it lands with AKOS rather than among the host's Home/Monitor/Operate. *@

    <RailHome />
    <RailMonitor />
    @* M11 S2 — the rail hides what the caller's roles cannot open. An AKOS-user key holds `*/read` minus
       Burbuja.Pomnibot and Burbuja.Compute, so a Pomnibot row is a row that lands on a refusal wall; the
       answer comes from the KERNEL's role definitions for this session (the release's roles are ARM
       resources, so a mirror of the built-ins alone cannot answer), and the entry is simply absent rather
       than disabled — a greyed row still promises a page. *@
    <RailOperate Pomnibot="@_pomnibot" />
    <RailCatalogue />
</nav>

@code {
    private bool _pomnibot = true;
    private bool _loaded;

    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (_loaded)
            return;
        _loaded = true;
        try
        {
            _pomnibot = await Data.MayAsync("Burbuja.Pomnibot/jobs/read", CancellationToken.None);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // The rail is navigation: a host that cannot answer shows everything rather than nothing, and the
            // page behind the row is where a refusal belongs.
        }

        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        // Nothing to release: the rail holds no subscription. Present so a fact can dispose a render tree
        // that includes it without the container complaining.
    }
}
